  "response": "Anthropologists who find the same practice in two distant societies must choose between two explanatory frameworks. Diffusionism is the view that cultural traits spread from one society to another through contact. Independent invention, by contrast, is the view that similar cultural traits can arise separately in unconnected societies. Neither position can be settled by the trait alone, because a shared practice is compatible with both histories. The choice becomes methodological as well as historical: comparative studies that treat every society as a separate data point overcount any trait that has in fact travelled. Although Francis Galton raised the problem of non-independent cultural cases at an 1889 lecture by Edward Tylor, Galton's problem was first formulated by Franz Boas. Whoever is credited with it, the problem forces researchers to look beyond the shared trait. They seek independent lines of evidence, such as linguistic, botanical, genetic and archaeological data, that can show whether the two societies were ever in contact, and when.\n\nThe sweet potato shows how such lines of evidence converge. The plant is native to the Americas, and its wild relatives and centre of domestication lie on that continent. Its presence in Polynesia therefore has to be explained, because its cultivation there could not have begun from a local wild ancestor. Linguistic data provide one route. Polynesian names for the sweet potato resemble the Quechua word \"cumal\", and that correspondence indicates that the sweet potato reached Polynesia before any European voyage to the Pacific. The rival account holds that the sweet potato first reached Polynesia aboard European ships. These two accounts cannot both be true, and one of them must hold: either the crop was established in the islands before Europeans entered the Pacific, or European vessels introduced it. Radiocarbon-dated tuber remains from Polynesian sites and the botanical record of early Pacific cultivars bear directly on which history is correct. Archaeobotanists Jon Hather and Patrick Kirch dated carbonized sweet potato remains from Mangaia in the Cook Islands to around 1000 CE and determined that the crop was established in Polynesia before any European arrival, settling the question against the European-ships account.\n\nEstablishing that a crop crossed the Pacific raises a further question about who carried it. At least one of two voyaging histories must hold, perhaps both. Polynesian voyagers may have reached the coast of South America, or Native American voyagers may have reached islands in eastern Polynesia. The navigational record makes the first route especially plausible, given the long-distance seafaring that carried Polynesians across most of the ocean. Genetic evidence now constrains the question further. A 2020 genomic study published in Nature concluded that Polynesians and Native Americans came into contact around 1200 CE, and Polynesian landfall on the South American coast confirms that conclusion. The genomic finding places the contact within a narrow window of time and thereby turns a long-debated possibility into a dated historical event.\n\nArchaeology supplies the most direct test. The decisive case is the Norse presence in the Americas. Helge and Anne Stine Ingstad began excavating L'Anse aux Meadows in 1960, six decades before the site's chronology was fixed with annual precision. L'Anse aux Meadows is located in Nova Scotia. Its location is a matter of geography alone, whereas tree-ring analysis dated woodcutting at L'Anse aux Meadows to 1021 CE, fixing the settlement in time rather than space. The analysis identified a cosmic-ray event preserved in the growth rings of timber cut with metal tools. The Norse reached North America roughly five centuries before Columbus. In other words, and because of that felling date, Norse voyagers were in the Americas about 500 years before Columbus's 1492 voyage. The excavated buildings, the iron rivets, and the worked wood leave no room for an argument from independent invention in this case.\n\nThese cases share a common structure. A similarity between distant societies becomes evidence of contact only when it is anchored by data that the similarity itself cannot produce. Such data include a borrowed word, a genome with mixed ancestry, or a securely dated structure built in a foreign style. Where those anchors are absent, independent invention remains the more cautious inference. Where they are present, diffusion is no longer a speculative hypothesis but a demonstrated historical event.",
